 "asked_at": "2026-10-18T00:45+08:00",
 "answer": "The air is better in the **north**. Both regions are in the \"unhealthy\" range because of the ongoing haze, but the west is noticeably worse.\n\nThese are the latest figures I found, from 2pm on Oct 17. The 24-hour PSI was in the \"unhealthy\" range in all regions: central (184), north (135), south (154), east (173) and west (185).\n\n- **North:** 24-hour PSI 135, and the hourly PM2.5 remained in the \"elevated\" range in the north at 91.\n- **West:** 24-hour PSI 185, and the hourly PM2.5 readings stood at 243 in the west. That's the highest of all five regions. The west also entered the \"high\" range at 158 at 11am, before any other region.\n\n**What the numbers mean:** The PSI is a 24-hour rolling average of six key air pollutants. Hourly PM2.5 is used as an indicator of current air quality. PM2.5 scores from 0 to 55 represent normal values, while readings between 56 and 150 indicate an elevated concentration of fine particles. Readings between 151 and 250 are considered high. That puts the west's current PM2.5 at nearly three times the north's.\n\nEven the north isn't healthy right now, so it's still a good idea to cut back on long or strenuous outdoor activity. Readings change hourly, so check the NEA's haze site (haze.gov.sg) or the myENV app for the newest numbers.",
